div.p:
'What day is this for me, ye gods I love? Indeed I greatly rejoice
that son and son's son strive together about virtue';
and: 'far better is he than his father.'"

"that I might make myself pitiable to my father." MURREDIUS, true to his usual stupidity, said that he had prepared a drug for sleep, because unceasing anxieties had made wakefulness habitual to him. From the father's side he gave a colour and a Publilian sententia: "His disownings," he said, "he dissolved with poison"; and again: "My death," he said, "he poured out." I remember OSCUS, when he was speaking of this kind of sententia, with which the talents of all the young men were already infected, complaining of PUBLILIUS, as though he had introduced this madness. CASSIUS SEVERUS, a great lover of Publilius, used to say that this fault was not his, but of those who imitated him in the part in which they ought to have passed him by, and did not imitate what in him was better said than by any comic or tragic writer, Roman or Greek; as that line, than which he said no better line could be found:
The miser lacks what he has as much as what he has not;
and that other on the same theme:
Luxury lacks many things, avarice all;
and those lines which could suit even this thrice-disowned man:
O life, long for the wretched, short for the happy!
